# Task: Weekly commit sync for code graphs and catalogs

## Context

Our internal code-intelligence platform is built on FastAPI, LangGraph, MongoDB and LanceDB, deployed on OpenShift (OCP). LLM calls go through an internal OpenAI-compatible gateway. Many components are already onboarded:
- their source was fetched as a GitHub zipball, because GitPython does not work in OCP
- graphifyy built a code graph for each
- the `generate_catalog` playbook produced each catalog, with `identify_component_type` classifying the component

Today a catalog is a one-time snapshot: it records no commit sha and never updates. Build a pipeline that, once a week per GitHub org, detects which onboarded components have new commits on their tracked branch. For each one it updates the graph and embeddings from the diff, reruns the catalog only when a catalog-relevant file changed, and stamps everything with the exact commit sha it reflects.

## Hard constraints

- **Pull only.** GitHub Enterprise webhooks and CI pipelines (Jenkins/Harness) cannot call any platform endpoint. All detection is the platform calling the GHE API.
- **No GitPython, no git binary.** Use only GHE REST and GraphQL:
  - compare API for diffs
  - contents API (raw) or zipball pinned to an exact sha for files
- **Reuse, don't reinvent.**
  - Use the existing MongoDB job queue and its global concurrency cap for sync jobs.
  - Call the existing graphifyy indexer, `generate_catalog` and `identify_component_type` playbooks, and LanceDB writer.
  - Add no new framework or abstraction layer beyond what these steps strictly need.
- **Additive schema only.** Existing component and catalog documents keep their shape; add fields and collections, never rename or drop.
- **`generate_catalog` stays as it is.** Rerun it whole when the gate fires. Do not split it into sections in this task. Token cost is out of scope.
- **Memory safety.** The main platform pod was previously OOMKilled by simultaneous graphifyy initialisation across 1000+ repos. Every graph build in this pipeline must go through the global concurrency cap.
- **Scale target.** About 1,000 orgs, up to 1,000,000 repos in total, of which about 50,000 are onboarded components. Never list all repos in an org for detection.

